  "onboarding.company.error.domain": "Give the website address of the company, for example orvayos.com.",
  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  // Refusing a company we will not work on.
  //
  // ONE LINE PER CATEGORY, and the categories are the closed union in
  // `packages/domain/src/acceptable-use.ts`. A test asserts a sentence exists
  // for every member, so a category can never be raised with nothing to say.
  //
  // The sentence NEVER names what matched. Telling somebody which term fired
  // hands them the list one probe at a time, and the person who typed it
  // innocently does not need to know either.
  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  "onboarding.refused.heading": "We do not work on this",
  "onboarding.refused.child_safety": "We will not set up a company for this, and we do not make exceptions.",
  "onboarding.refused.sexual_content": "We do not work on sexual content or sexual services.",
  "onboarding.refused.intrusion": "We do not work on malware or intrusion tooling.",
  "onboarding.refused.fraud": "We do not work on fraud.",
  "onboarding.refused.controlled_goods": "We do not work on the sale of weapons or controlled substances.",
  "onboarding.refused.harassment": "We do not work on targeted harassment of a person.",
  "onboarding.refused.terms": "Read the acceptable use terms",
  // The escape hatch, because a short denylist WILL refuse somebody wrongly and
  // a refusal with no way forward is the loop this flow already had once.
  "onboarding.refused.mistake": "If this is a mistake, the name is what was checked, not you. Change it and carry on.",
  // The same way out when the refused box was the website address, which is
  // checked too: telling somebody to change a name that was fine leads nowhere.
  "onboarding.refused.mistake-domain": "If this is a mistake, the website address is what was checked, not you. Change it and carry on.",
  // The marketing site
