  "studio.offline.reason": "The build service is not connected here.",
  // The seven ways a request stops before it becomes a decision.
  //
  // They are the other half of the pair `outcome.*` opened. A DECISION has three
  // exact sentences and has had them since the policy engine shipped; a request
  // that never became a decision had none, so all of these rendered as
  // "the builder cannot reach the build service". That sentence is true of
  // exactly one of them and is wrong about the rest, and it is wrong in
  // the expensive direction: somebody whose address was malformed was handed a
  // fact about our infrastructure at the moment they were looking for a fact
  // about their own typing.
  //
  // THE LAST TWO ARE THE TWO CEILINGS, AND THEY ARE NOT THE SAME SENTENCE.
  // A model call is refused before it is made by whichever of two limits is
  // reached first: the customer's own plan, or the deployment cap that bounds
  // what the business can be billed across every company at once. Both used to
  // read as "the build service did not answer", which was false about a service
  // that had answered in nine milliseconds and said which one it was.
  //
  //   - `allowance` is theirs. It is the one a customer can act on, so it says
  //     what ran out and what changes it.
  //   - `unavailable` is ours. It names no number and no limit, because a spend
  //     ceiling published to a customer is a fact about our bill and about what
  //     it costs to take this surface down. It says the thing that IS theirs to
  //     know, which is that they were not charged and it was not their fault.
  //     A translator must not helpfully add a cause here.
  //
  // EVERY ONE OF THEM SAYS WHAT DID NOT HAPPEN, and a translator must keep that
  // half exactly as blunt as the English. "Nothing has been spent", "nothing was
  // published", "nothing changed" is the half a customer needs, for the same
  // reason `studio.offline.body` states it: this product bills for model calls,
  // and a refusal that leaves somebody unsure whether they were charged is a
  // refusal that costs a support message.
  "studio.refused.address": "Use three or more letters, numbers or hyphens, starting with a letter.",
  "studio.refused.brief": "Say what the site is for first. Nothing spent.",
  "studio.refused.replacement": "Type the new words, or describe removing them.",
  "studio.refused.text": "Those words are not on the page, or appear more than once.",
  "studio.refused.records": "Your company records did not load. Nothing ran.",
  // WHY THIS IS NOT A FAULT SENTENCE. Every build made before Orvay kept
  // per-version copies has a history row and nothing to restore from, so a site
  // that has been building for a while will show several. The words say the
  // cause is permanent and say what the customer CAN do, because a refusal that
  // reads like a fault sends somebody to check a service that is working.
  "studio.refused.no-snapshot": "No saved copy of this build. Later ones can be restored.",
  "studio.refused.build-failed": "It did not compile. Nothing spent. Describe the change and build again.",
  "studio.refused.unusable": "No usable site came back. Credits were used. Say more and try again.",
  "studio.refused.build-running": "Still building. Publish when it finishes.",
  // R2.5: a second Publish while the first may still be under way.
  "studio.refused.publish-running": "Already publishing. Reload in a minute.",
  "studio.refused.publish-withdrawn": "The approval was withdrawn.",
  "studio.refused.build-service": "The build service did not answer. Nothing published or spent.",
  "studio.refused.allowanceShort": "A build needs more credits than are left. Nothing used. Credits reset next period.",
  "studio.refused.allowance": "Credits are used up this period. Nothing spent.",
  "studio.refused.unavailable": "The builder is busy on our side. Nothing spent. Try again later.",
  // WHAT IS WAITING, AND WHERE THE DECISION IS MADE.
  //
  // `outcome.requires_approval` renders as "Waiting for a human", which is a
  // true and complete description of the state and names no person, no queue and
  // no screen. On its own it is a dead end presented as a status, and it invites
  // a reader to believe the request went to a colleague. It did not: the gate
  // answered, the request was refused before anything was created, and there is
  // nowhere in the product yet to record an approval for this.
  //
  // TWO KEYS RATHER THAN A SENTENCE WITH A LINK INSIDE IT. Splitting a sentence
  // around an anchor puts the punctuation inside the link in one language and
  // outside it in the next, and word order moves the fragment somewhere the
  // English never put it. `auth.assent.*` is four keys for the same reason. The
  // second key is a whole clause, so every language can shape it freely.
  //
  // "Policies" IS THE SCREEN'S OWN NAME AND STAYS AS IT IS DRAWN. The rail in
  // `apps/app` labels that destination in English in every locale today, so a
  // translated link label would send a reader looking for a word that is not on
  // their screen. Treat it as a proper noun until the rail is translated too.
  "studio.refused.approval": "A person must approve this first, and nobody was asked. Nothing published or spent.",
  "studio.refused.approval.link": "Open Policies to see what runs without asking",
  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  // THE ADDRESS LOCKS THE MOMENT THE COMPANY HOLDS IT.
  //
  // `studio.address.hint` used to promise a customer they could change the
  // address until it was published, and that promise is withdrawn rather than
  // softened. `orvay.sites` makes a label belong to exactly one company, and it
  // does so at the first build, so the sentence describing what happens then has
  // to describe the claim rather than the publish. A hint that still offered a
  // rename would be false at the one moment somebody relied on it.
  //
  // ONE LOCK SENTENCE, FOR A PUBLISHED ADDRESS ONLY (2026-10-21). "Links point
  // at it" is a reason only once something has been published; said of an
  // address nobody has visited it would be the §12b failure in its quietest
  // form. A reserved address is drawn as text where the field was, with no
  // sentence.
  "studio.address.opens": "Opens the published site in a new tab.",
