  // Every answer lost words the request did not ask to change, so none was kept (`keptText`, 2026-10-05).
  "studio.refused.lost-text": "Each attempt removed words you did not ask to change. Credits were used.",
  "studio.badge.label": "Built with Orvay",
  "studio.badge.hide": "Hide this mark until the page loads again",
  "studio.address.published": "Published, so it cannot change. Links point at it.",
  // The two ways an address can refuse, and neither of them is an outage.
  //
  // `address-taken` is somebody else holding the name. It has to say the work
  // survives, because it does: a draft is stored under a key derived from the
  // company and has nothing to do with the address, so choosing another name
  // keeps everything the model wrote. A customer who reads this and believes
  // their site is gone will not try a second name.
  //
  // `address-frozen` is this company already holding a published one. Both say
  // nothing was spent, and both are true because the address is claimed before
  // the model is called rather than after it answers.
  "studio.refused.address-taken": "Another company has it. Nothing spent. Choose another; your work is kept.",
  "studio.refused.address-frozen": "A published address cannot move. Nothing built or spent.",
  // THE ONE REFUSAL ON THIS SCREEN THAT MAY NOT PROMISE NOTHING WAS SPENT.
  //
  // A build that stopped being observed part of the way through may have reached
  // the model or may never have left, and nothing in the record can tell the two
  // apart. Every other sentence here says "nothing was spent" because it is
  // checkable; this one says it may have been, because that is what is known. A
  // translator must not tidy the hedge away, and must not turn it into a
  // reassurance either.
  "studio.refused.interrupted": "It may have finished, and may have used credits. Results show next time.",
  // A BUILD THE SERVICE LOST AND COULD NOT PICK UP AGAIN, in two shapes: it was picked up as often as forge allows
  // (`MAX_RESUMES`) and lost again, or too much time had passed to pick it up at all. Unlike `interrupted` above,
  // it is KNOWN here that the build will not finish, so both say so. The hedge about credits stays and must stay in
  // translation: a call the lost build had made may have been billed, and nothing can say whether it was.
  "studio.refused.interrupted-limit": "Interrupted too often. It may have used credits. Build again.",
  "studio.refused.interrupted-expired": "Interrupted too long to resume. It may have used credits. Build again.",
  // WHAT THE STOPPED BUILD DID TO THE WORKING DRAFT, appended to the sentence above only when forge's record says
  // (`draftSaved`). Two whole sentences rather than a clause, so no sentence promises the draft is unchanged when
  // the build may have rewritten it.
  "studio.refused.draft-saved": "A new draft was saved before it stopped.",
  "studio.refused.draft-unchanged": "Nothing was saved. Your draft is unchanged.",
  // The acceptable-use refusal, which used to read as an outage.
  //
  // It says what will not happen and does not say which rule decided, because a
  // refusal that recites its own test is a refusal somebody can walk around.
  "studio.refused.prohibited": "{brand} will not build this. Nothing spent.",
  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  // A BUILD THAT REPORTS ITSELF.
  //
  // A real build took three minutes in the build service and an earlier one was
  // cancelled at just under two. The screen used to show one word for the whole
  // of that, so these six sentences exist to make a long wait legible.
  //
  // EVERY ONE OF THEM IS IN THE PAST EXCEPT THE SECOND, and that is the design
  // rather than an inconsistency. Each line is written when the thing has already
  // happened, so a line that promised work in progress would be a progress bar
  // with nothing behind it. The exception is the model, which is recorded as it
  // starts precisely because it is the step that explains a three-minute silence,
  // and its sentence says so plainly instead of leaving somebody to wonder.
  "studio.progress.label": "What the build has done so far",
